==English==

===Etymology===
From the {{etyl|fr|en}} {{m|fr|tenable}}, from {{m|fr|tenir||to hold}}; compare {{m|en|tenible}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈtɛn.ə.bəl/|lang=en}}
{{rhymes|ɛnəbəl|lang=en}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# {{lb|en|of a theory, argument, etc.}} capable of being [[maintained]] or [[justified]]; [[well-founded]]
#: ''Back in the 1800s, many did not consider Darwin's theory of evolution to be '''tenable''' at all.''
#* {{quote-book| title=The Covenant of Peace: An Essay on the League of Nations| page=25| url=https://books.google.com/books?id=qWFJAAAAIAAJ| author=Henry Noel Brailsford| year=1919| year=Modern war rests on industry, and its evolution has all but abolished the "non-combatant," emptied the old idea of "innocent" trade of meaning, and made even of neutrality a barely '''tenable''' status.}}
#* {{quote-book| title=Making Friends: The Influences of Culture and Development| page=391| url=https://books.google.com/books?id=wSLaAAAAMAAJ| author=Luanna H. Meyer| year=1998| passage=Fitting into a '''tenable''' social position among other people requires not only the ability to simultaneously assess the nature of relationships with each person individually but also the ability to comprehend the indirect relational consequences of actions for connected relationships.}}
#* {{quote-news|work=The Tufts Daily| title=Op-Ed: A kinder space in the ivory tower| author=Maxwell Bevilacqua| date=February 8, 2017 | passage=In short, it is not a '''tenable''' position in our tight-knit community, or in a pluralistic democracy, to ask for a kinder space and to be unkind towards others.}}
# {{lb|en|military}} Capable of being [[defended]] against [[assault]] or [[attack]]; [[defensible]]
#* {{quote-book|title=A History of Greece; from the Earliest Period to the Close of the Generation Contemporary with Alexander the Great| volume=8| author={{w|George Grote}}| 
url=https://books.google.com/books?id=I81fdRtI804C| year=1850| passage=The island of Melos undoubtedly fell within his general conceptions of '''tenable''' empire for Athens.}}
# Fit for [[habitation]], similar, or related use.
#* {{quote-book|title=The ancestors and descendants of Theodore Munroe Foote| year=1679|  page=14| url=https://books.google.com/books?id=yFhMAAAAMAAJ| author=Roberta Elizabeth Foote| year_published=1969| passage=In February, 1679, he was admitted "a planter" of the town and a home lot was granted him "on condition that it should have a '''tenable''' house built upon it within two years.}}
#* {{quote-book|title=The White Flag| page=387| url=https://books.google.com/books?id=qxgbaxP6dngC| author=Gene Stratton-Porter| year=1923| passage=They made a day of straightening the leaning stable on its foundations and staying its framework, so that with new roof and sheathing, it would be a '''tenable''' building for many years to come.}}
#* {{quote-book|title=Consulting-specifying Engineer| volume=21-22| page=220| url=https://books.google.com/books?id=iuwRAQAAMAAJ| year=1997| passage=Protecting or assisting occupant evacuation and safety by maintaining '''tenable''' building spaces and exit paths}}

